### 1.1 `when` (item 1)

#### 1.1.1 Syntax accepted

No new token. `when` is already a contextual identifier read only after a rule body
(`parser_rule.cpp`); the accepted form is

```camel
rule add_zero(x: int, y: int) { x + y => x } when isZero(y)
```

`expr` is an ordinary Camel expression. It must have type `bool`. It may call functions of the
module, use the rule's parameters, and read the graph module. There is no `when` on an `analysis`;
no `where`; no new precedence.

`rule<...>` (a rule over functions) stays refused with `ConstructNotSupported`.

#### 1.1.2 What the declaration compiles to

A rule with a condition becomes four functions instead of three:

```camel
func add_zero__lhs(x: int, y: int) => x + y     // the pattern
func add_zero__rhs(x: int, y: int) => x         // what it becomes
func add_zero__when(x: Node, y: Node) => isZero(y)   // the condition, parameters as nodes
func add_zero() => __rule_when('add_zero', false, 0, 0, add_zero__lhs, add_zero__rhs, add_zero__when)
```

* Each parameter of the condition has the **same name and position** as the rule's parameter, and
  the type `graph::Node`. A `const` parameter is a `Node` too; its bit stays in `consts`.
  Defaults are not copied: the engine always supplies the argument.
* The condition's return type is written as `bool` so that a condition that is not a `bool`
  fails where it is written, naming `add_zero__when`.
* `__rule_when` is a new entity of `modules/rewrite`, `OperatorGroup` `__rule_when`, operator URI
  `rewrite:rule_when`, 7 normal parameters. The 6-argument `__rule` stays, so a rule without a
  condition produces the same AST as before (the existing AST and format tests are unchanged).
  The declaration imports `__rule_when` from `rewrite` and `Node` from `graph` once per module,
  as it already imports `__rule` once.
* The `__rule_when` type resolver requires: `string, bool, int, int, F, F, F`, `F` a function
  type, and the last function's exit type is `bool`. At `compileRule` time the runtime also
  checks that the condition's normal parameter count equals the left side's; a mismatch is a
  `RuleCompileError` naming the rule.

#### 1.1.3 What a pattern variable is: scheme (a)

Inside `name__when`, parameter *i* is a `graph::Node` that names the node the engine bound to
parameter *i* for this match: `Match.vars[i]` in the draft of `Match.writings.draft()`. That
draft is the graph being rewritten at that moment (the decoded copy of the graph version plus
the rewrites made so far), not the frozen program and not the rule's own side graphs.

Consequences:

* A condition is evaluated **after** a match and **before** the replacement, at rewrite time
  (the engine sites in `writings.cpp` already call `rule.when` there).
* A parameter the left side never uses has no bound node. Its handle names no node; reading it
  faults. Such a condition cannot be written meaningfully, which is acceptable because a rule
  with such a parameter is already refused on its right side.
* A `const` parameter is matched by a *constant writing*, which need not be a DATA node; the
  condition sees the matched node. `graph::is_constant`/`constant` keep their frozen-graph
  meaning ("this node is a constant"), so an author who needs the value of a `const` parameter
  should use a literal or `graph::constant` on the node that is the literal. This is open
  question 1.
* The condition has no variable for the whole matched node (the root of the left side). A
  first version does not add one; the root's operator is already known from the rule's own left
  side, and its type can be read from any parameter of the same operator family. If needed later,
  a reserved parameter (e.g. `this: Node`) is a separate decision.

#### 1.1.4 Reading the draft: the graph module's handles

`Handle` gets a `const GraphDraft *draft_` (null for every handle that names a frozen program).
A new `Handle::createInDraft(draft, nodeRef, allocator)` names a node of the live draft; it
stores no `Program` (the session's work program is not published and need not be rooted for a
read). `Handle::equals` and `clone` include the draft. `Handle::updateRefs` still traces the
program when there is one.

The node readers are taught both sources with a small `NodeView` (frozen `GCNode` or
`DraftNodeHeader`, same answer): `inputs`, `with_inputs`, `ctrl_inputs`, `users`,
`value_users`, `is_constant`, `kind`, `op`, `op_name`, `is_pure`, `type_of`, `type_name`,
`tags`, `is_anchor`, `is_written_slot`, `origin`, `show`, `constant`.

* `users`/`value_users` on a draft merge the draft's norm/with/ctrl user lists, sorted and
  de-duplicated, as the frozen path does.
* `is_written_slot` on a draft builds `WrittenSlots` from the draft (the same helper the frozen
  path uses); it is not cached per draft revision in the first version (a condition that asks it
  per match is O(n); a node-level fact cache is the fix, H39 item 4).
* `callee` returns null on a draft: a `Graph` handle names a version of the program, and a draft
  handle carries none. No rule pattern can match a call today, so no condition needs it.
* The Program and Graph reads (`graphs`, `root`, `name`, `nodes`) are unchanged: a condition gets
  no Program handle, only Nodes.
* `constant(n, sample)` on a draft: DATA node, static slot, no inputs, value of the requested
  type, exactly as on a frozen graph.

The graph module's draft path is part of the handle contract, so `docs/technical/45_graph_module.md`
and `46_rules.md` record which reads answer from a draft.

#### 1.1.5 How the condition runs

`sourceOf` (`modules/rewrite/module.cpp`) builds `RuleSource.when` when the rule object has a
condition:

1. It roots the `RuleObject` in a `mm::RootHandle` owned by a small shared struct captured by the
   closure. The rule object traces the condition function, whose Program traces its graph and the
   literals in the condition. A NodeVM call is a safepoint, so the closure re-reads the
   `Function *` from the rooted object after the last allocation and immediately before the call.
2. Per match it reads `GraphDraft &draft = match.writings.draft()`, creates one Node handle per
   condition parameter (`match.vars[i]`, or an invalid ref when the variable is unbound), and
   roots each handle with a `mm::RootHandle` (they are auto-space objects).
3. It calls `NodeVMSchedPass::callGraph(whenFunction->program(), whenFunction->graph(), args)`
   and interprets the slot as `Bool`.
4. One `NodeVMSchedPass` is made per engine call and shared by all the rules of that call; it
   does not outlive the call (`NodeVMSchedPass` already roots its frames).

The engine itself needs no change: it already consults `rule.when` at the greedy match
(`writings.cpp`, `rewrite`) and at the saturation match (`saturate`). A rule applies at a match
exactly when the condition returns true. A fault inside the condition propagates as the
`rewrite`/`saturate` error value `RewriteInvalid`; it is never treated as false.

`check` (`checkKernel`) changes for conditional rules: it skips the unconditional comparison of
the two sides for random inputs (the condition is part of the equation, and the tool cannot
evaluate it on the sampled values) and relies on the engine path it already has: apply the rule
to its own left side, require it to fire, and compare before/after. A condition that is false on
every generated instance makes the rule report `never fired`; the tool cannot invent inputs that
satisfy a condition yet (open question 3).

#### 1.1.6 Garbage collection

A condition call may allocate and collect, so everything the engine and the closure hold across
it must be rooted or relocated:

* the `RuleObject` and, through it, the condition function: rooted (above);
* the Node arguments: rooted by the per-match `RootHandle`s and by the VM frame;
* the draft's **static values** (`std::vector<slot_t>`): not traced today. New
  `GraphDraft::relocateStaticValues(relocate)` relocates every GC-traced static slot.
* the **constant slots copied into the compiled `Pattern`s** (`patterns` are C++ values, not
  graph objects; a string constant would go stale): `Pattern::value` becomes `mutable` and
  `runtime::traceRuleConstants(rules, relocate)` relocates every constant/computed argument.
  The rule's Program keeps the objects alive; the trace only has to update the copies.
* `DraftWritings::rewrite` and `saturate` register one external-root tracer for the duration of
  the engine call (owner: a local RAII object) that does both. It is registered always, not only
  when a rule has a condition: the cost is two walks per collection while an engine runs, and
  there is no collection while it runs without a Camel call.
* the `NodeVMSchedPass` shared by the conditions: a C++ object whose frame pool registers its own
  tracer; it lives as long as the engine call.

The global `NodeVMSchedPass` and `NoCollectionScope` rules are respected: no collection scope is
put around the engine or the calls.

#### 1.1.7 Limits and diagnostics

* `when` is accepted; `rule<...>` remains `ConstructNotSupported` at the location of `<`.
* The condition is type-checked as a normal function; a non-`bool` body is a located type error
  in `name__when`.
* Side effects are not enforced statically. The design forbids them ("`when` runs at rewrite
  time, it cannot have side effects"); the language has no purity annotation for arbitrary
  functions, so a `println` in a condition prints during the pass. The rule's `check` and review
  remain the guard. Open question 2.
* `when` on a `<=>` rule applies to both directions (the same closure, the same parameter order,
  because both sides have the same parameters).
* The condition is per match, so a condition over a graph read is O(1) except `is_written_slot`
  (above). A native fast path for shape/type tests is future work, not a first-version goal.

