### 2.2 How the condition runs

`sourceOf` builds `RuleSource.when` from the rule object's third function. The closure is a
`shared_ptr` struct (`WhenCall`) that owns the `RootHandle` of the `RuleObject` and one
`NodeVMSchedPass`. Per match it reads `match.writings.draft()`, creates one `Node` handle per
condition parameter (`match.vars[i]`, or an invalid ref when the left side never bound it), roots
each handle with an `mm::RootHandle`, re-reads the function from the rooted rule and calls
`callGraph(when->program(), when->graph(), args)`; the `Bool` in the result slot is the answer. A
rule applies exactly when that answer is true. A fault inside the condition is a `RewriteInvalid`
error value, never `false`.

`EngineRootScope` (an RAII local in `DraftWritings::rewrite`/`saturate`) registers
`rewrite.engineRoots` with `mm::autoSpace()`: while the engine runs, a collection relocates the
draft's static values (`GraphDraft::relocateStaticValues`) and the constant/computed slots copied
into the compiled `Pattern`s (`runtime::traceRuleConstants`). The Node arguments are rooted, the
rule object is rooted, and the shared `NodeVMSchedPass` registers its own frame tracer. No
`NoCollectionScope` is put around a user call.

`check` for a conditional rule skips the sampled comparison of the two sides (the tool cannot
evaluate a condition on sampled values), fires the rule on its own left side, and compares
before/after; its report line is `ok name: the condition selects the instances, fired k of m`. A
conditional rule whose sides disagree is still reported (`FAIL name: the rewritten left side
differs`), and a condition that rejects every generated instance reports `never fired`.

