### 2.5 A pass in the program's own module (fixed)

`camel prog.cml prog::apply` — one module that holds the rule, the target and the `export func
apply` the pass list names — used to end in a null program graph. Before this fix the Release
binary died with `0xC0000005`; the baseline binary from `D:\Projects\Camel\out\latest` dies the
same way on the same file, so the defect is develop's, not H38's. (An earlier note in this report
called it "exit 0, no output"; that is what a build that tolerated the null graph would show, and
what the `stage_`-done module amounts to. The builds here crash instead, which is the same root
cause with a different last step.)

What happens, exactly:

1. A Camel pass is a program of its own built from the module by `compileAsProgram`
   (`src/core/module/userdef.cpp`). The module is also the program the CLI is about to compile, so
   the tool program and the user's program are two `Builder`s over one `UserDefinedModule`.
2. `Builder::buildFrom` calls `userModule->setProgramGraph(root)` at the end of the build. Its
   `buildingProgramOfItsOwn_` guard is right to keep the tool program's root away from
   `compileGraph_` (the graph the module has in the program that loaded it). But the guard also
   did `stage_ = CompileStage::Done; loaded_ = true;`, and `compileAsProgram` did the same at its
   end.
3. So the entry module was marked done while its own program graph had never been built.
   `mainModule->compile(CompileStage::Done)` starts with `if (till <= stage_) return true;` and
   returns: the program is never built.
4. `Context::runtimeProgram()` then materializes `mainModule->compileGraph_`, which is null.
   `encodeToRuntimeProgram` dereferences it: `ASSERT(rootGraph_ != nullptr)` in Debug,
   `0xC0000005` in Release, from `tools/camel-cli/main.cpp:456`.

The fix keeps the guard and the stage marks apart: `isTheProgramBeingCompiled()` (`context_ &&
context_->mainModule().get() == this`) says whether this module is the entry module whose program
has not been built yet. `setProgramGraph` and `compileAsProgram` take the `stage_`/`loaded_` marks
for every module **except** that one; the entry module stays at `GCT` and its own `compile(Done)`
then declares it into the context builder and builds it. For pass modules that are not the entry
module nothing changes (they are marked done as before), and neither does the imported-module
handling of develop's newer `compileAsProgram`.

The case that locks it is `test/cases/modules/rules/same_module_pass.cml`: a module with a rule,
a target and `export func apply`. The rule is deliberately **not** an equation (`x + 0 => x + 1`)
so the two programs print different values: `camel same_module_pass.cml same_module_pass::apply`
prints `8`, `camel same_module_pass.cml std::nvm` prints `7`. The first test in
`test/plans/feat/modules/rules.plan.toml` would have crashed before the fix and cannot pass on an
empty or unrewritten program; the second says which value belongs to the program as written.
`std::gir` confirms the rewrite independently: after the pass, `f` is `add(x, 1)` (the constant the
right side builds), not the `add(x, 0)` of the source. Node and operator counts stay the same
because the rule replaces one constant by one constant, which is why the two outputs are the
evidence the plan uses.

Why the first attempt at this bug failed: it was not attempted. The defect was recorded and the
rule tests were moved to the two-module layout, which was the wrong call — the shape is a natural
way to write a rewriting pass. The later attempt to fix it went wrong in the tree, not in the
diagnosis: the fix was written on top of the `when` commit whose rebased `read.cpp` no longer had
the draft reads (the coordinator's conflict resolution had taken develop's side for the whole
file), and the draft support was then merged back against `develop`'s `read.cpp` while HEAD did
not have develop's `regionkind.*` files. The result was an unbuildable `read.cpp` that included
`regionkind.h` without the branch that has it. The branch is now rebased onto current develop
(`80cace9b`, after the loop-regions merge `348bbf92`) and `read.cpp` was rebuilt as a three-way
merge of my full version onto **develop's** file, with develop's arm semantics kept (an anchor is
exit, output or return; arm heads and tails are regions, not anchors), and the same-module fix was
re-applied on develop's newer `compileAsProgram`.

